00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.810 align:start position:0%
 
you  [Music]  in our morning rounds is Breast Cancer  Awareness Month this october draws to a  close we're focusing on the unintended  consequences of breast cancer treatment  more than 3.8 million breast cancer  survivors live in the united states  recent advancements in early detection  and treatment really have saved many  lives but those treatments may also  increase a woman's risk of  cardiovascular disease in fact it's  heart disease not cancer reoccurrence  that is the number one cause of death  for breast cancer survivors and women  overall dr. tara nurullah is a  cardiologist at northwell health joins  us more on this I think a lot of people  will be surprised to hear this Tara good  morning to you good morning Gail yes in  about 10% of breast cancer patients the  same chemotherapy and radiation  treatments that zap cancer cells can  also damage the heart it can happen  during treatment or years afterward now  an emerging field called cardio oncology  has evolved to protect the hearts of  cancer patients both during and after  treatment to make sure they live full  lives after defeating cancer four months  into her fight against stage 2 breast  cancer  Andrea Chen Frannie learned she had a  new health challenge to conquer it  really did feel like another punch you  know that where you've been punched a  couple times after recovering from a  mastectomy andrea received chemotherapy  to attack her breast cancer not knowing  that it was also attacking her heart it  was a little bit of a surprise  especially because I wasn't feeling  anything you were otherwise young and  healthy had no history of any heart  problems no no everything was was fine  an echocardiogram revealed the 40 year  old had heart failure and was among the  tens of thousands of breast cancer  patients who develop heart disease each  year dr. Chau dang is Andrea's  oncologist at Memorial sloan-kettering  during treatment we actually can see a  cardiac dysfunction namely heart failure  for some where our patients dr. Tang  teamed up with a cardiologist  prescribing medication to protect  Andrea's heart so she would be strong  enough to finish chemotherapy so the  feel of Cardiology is  a feel that is growing I think we're all  working very well together to take care  of our patients not only to improve  their cancer outcomes as they survive  but to improve their cardiovascular  outcomes as they age breast cancer  treatment puts the heart at risk in  several ways chemotherapy can damage the  heart muscle that pumps blood leading to  heart failure and radiation can disrupt  normal heart rhythm and damage both the  lining around the heart and the heart  valves but the biggest risk from  radiation is the development of early  and accelerated coronary artery disease  which raises the risk of heart attack  while some women have no symptoms others  experience shortness of breath chest  pain or decreased ability to exercise  what we should not be telling our  patients is don't get the very  treatments that make you beat the cancer  in the first place  dr. Javed Mosley he directs the cardio  oncology program at Vanderbilt  University the treatments are effective  but now the issue is how do we give the  drugs more safely but how do we prevent  heart disease that can develop as a  result of the very treatments that save  a patient's life at what point would a  woman be at risk of being affected by  the treatment she's receiving per breast  cancer it can happen years to even a  decade after completion of treatment and  it is this part that I think we as a  medical community need to raise more  awareness and do a better job of  screening for three years after  completing treatment Andrea's heart is  strong again but she will be monitored  for any lingering effects of the  chemotherapy for the rest of her life  once they've completed treatment cancer  patients should ask doctors to give them  a detailed record of their chemo and  radiation dosages to discuss with their  primary care doctor or cardiologist they  should also speak to them about how to  recognize symptoms of heart disease and  take preventive measures to lower their  risk I do feel for Andrea because it  does seem like a one-two punch and you  mentioned she didn't feel any symptoms  so how do you know if you're at risk  well the big key is awareness awareness  awareness on the clinician and providers  side in fact the American Heart  Association put out its first scientific  stage  in 2018 about the intersection of breast  cancer and cardiovascular disease and  awareness on the patient side so they  should know what their cardiovascular  risk factors are how much and what type  of chemotherapy or targeted therapy or  hormone therapy they got how much  radiation they received and have this  conversation with their doctor because  we're very good about kind of  shepherding patients through their  cancer treatment and really monitoring  them but it's that period afterwards  when they leave when they become a  survivor that we really don't do  something right kendricks for other  types of cancers affect the heart too or  is it just for breast cancer absolutely  you know so many people have said  they've never even heard of this field  of cardio oncology but this is a big  field and it's not just breast cancer it  is chemotherapy and other agents used  for different types of cancers radiation  you know patients who've had Hodgkin's  disease or may have gotten radiation to  the chest for that so this is a big  field so always good to know your chemo  or radiation history and just discuss it  with your doctor Tara thanks for raising  our awareness and I'm sure we appreciate  it

